  "text": "**Checking the three candidates against compact examples**\n\nSolutions 1 and 2 can be ruled out with a compact surface, since a compact surface is a special case of $V$ with $D=\\emptyset$, so $\\overline P_1 = p_g$ and $\\overline q = q$.\n\nTake a properly elliptic fibration $f: X \\to C$ over a curve of genus $b$ with $\\chi(\\mathcal O_X)=0$ and a non-trivial torsion line bundle in the Weierstrass-type data. Then $\\kappa(X)=1\\ge 0$, $q(X)=b$, $p_g(X)=b-1$, and the Albanese image is the curve $C$, so $X$ does not have maximal Albanese dimension. Such surfaces exist for every $b$.\n\n- For $b=3$: $p_g = 2 < 3 = 2q-3$, so Solution 1 fails.\n- For $b=3$: $p_g = 2 < 4 = 2q-2$, so Solution 2 fails. The same example also rules out any bound of the form $2q-4$ for $q \\ge 4$.\n\nSolution 3's bound $f(q)=q-1$ is consistent with this family, since the elliptic example with $\\chi=0$ gives $p_g = q-1$ exactly, so the bound is attained there. Solution 3's argument is weak: the claimed inequality $\\overline\\chi(V)\\ge 0$ for $\\overline\\kappa\\ge 0$ is asserted without justification, and the case analysis for higher-genus fibrations is hand-waved. I cannot verify the open-surface sharpness from first principles here. But among the three, $q-1$ is the only candidate that survives the compact test, and the question's wording (\"known sharp\") fits it.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
